{"answer":"Per the PostgreSQL 17.11 documentation:

**69.1. Backup Manifest Top-level Object** — The backup manifest is a JSON document whose top-level keys include: a version integer (2 beginning in PostgreSQL 17, 1 in older versions); the database system identifier of the instance where the backup was taken, present only when the manifest version is 2; a list of objects each describing one file present in the backup (no entries for needed WAL files or for the manifest itself); a list of objects each describing a range of WAL records that must be readable from a particular timeline to use the backup; and, always on the last line, a SHA256 checksum of all preceding lines (a fixed checksum method chosen to permit incremental parsing by clients).

**69.3. Backup Manifest WAL Range Object** — Each WAL range object always has three keys: the timeline for the range (an integer); the Start-LSN, the LSN at which replay must begin on that timeline to use the backup, stored in PostgreSQL's usual format (two hexadecimal strings of 1–8 characters separated by a slash); and the End-LSN, the earliest LSN at which replay on that timeline may end when using the backup, in the same format. Ordinarily there is only a single WAL range, but a backup taken from a standby that switches timelines during the backup due to an upstream promotion can have multiple ranges with different timelines; there are never multiple WAL ranges for the same timeline.","citations":["E01","E02"]}

