{"answer":"For both PostgreSQL 17.11 and 18.6, section 25.3.4 "Making a Base Backup Using the Low Level API" describes the same procedure. The text states that instead of using pg_basebackup, you can take a base backup via the low-level API; the procedure has a few more steps but is relatively simple, and it is very important that the steps be executed in sequence with each step's success verified before proceeding. Multiple backups can run concurrently, whether started via this API or via pg_basebackup.

The enumerated steps are identical in both versions: (1) ensure WAL archiving is enabled and working; (2) connect as a user with rights to run pg_backup_start (superuser or a user granted EXECUTE) and issue SELECT pg_backup_start(label => 'label', fast => false); the calling connection must be maintained until the backup ends or the backup is automatically aborted; online backups start at the beginning of a checkpoint, and by default pg_backup_start waits for the next scheduled checkpoint (governed by checkpoint_timeout and checkpoint_completion_target), while passing true requests an immediate checkpoint; (3) perform the backup with a file-system tool such as tar or cpio (not pg_dump or pg_dumpall), without stopping normal database operation, referring to Section 25.3.4.1 for considerations.

No differences between the 17.11 and 18.6 text for this section are present in the supplied evidence.","citations":["E01","E02","E03","E04"]}

